In a circuit, a 15 V battery, a key K and an ammeter A are joined in series with two groups of resistors: a 10 Ω and a 15 Ω resistor joined in parallel with each other, and a 60 Ω and a 40 Ω resistor joined in parallel with each other. The two parallel groups are in series with each other. Calculate:
(a) the total resistance of the circuit;
(b) the total current drawn from the source;
(c) the potential difference across the parallel combination of the 10 Ω and 15 Ω resistors. (3)

Think about each situation and describe its likely result: (5)
(i) a population of bacteria living in temperate waters, when the water temperature rises because of global warming;
(ii) the sperm meets the egg after reaching the oviduct of a human female;
(iii) self-pollination cannot happen in a flower that has only a pistil;
(iv) the egg is not fertilised in a human female;
(v) a seed is placed in soil with the right conditions of water and air.

(i) In a circuit, two 2 Ω resistors are joined in series. A 4 Ω resistor is joined across this pair (in parallel with the two 2 Ω resistors together). This combination is joined in series with a 3 Ω resistor, an ammeter, a 10 V battery and a key K. Calculate:
(I) the total resistance of the circuit;
(II) the total current drawn from the source;
(III) the potential difference across the 3 Ω resistor. (3)
(ii) Two bulbs rated 100 W; 220 V and 60 W; 220 V are joined in parallel to a 220 V mains supply. Calculate the current drawn from the mains. (2)

Think about each situation and describe what would happen when: (5)
(i) spores are released from the blob-like structures of bread mould;
(ii) leaves of Bryophyllum fall on wet soil;
(iii) pollen of one species lands on the stigma of a completely unrelated species;
(iv) a Copper-T is placed in the uterus of a human female;
(v) a mature Spirogyra breaks up into smaller pieces.

Write the chemical equations for the reactions that take place when: (1+1)
(i) manganese dioxide is heated with aluminium powder;
(ii) steam is passed over red-hot iron.

In respiration, glucose is first broken down into pyruvate. Write the end products made when pyruvate is broken down further in each case:
(a) in yeast, when there is no oxygen;
(b) in muscles, when oxygen is short;
(c) in mitochondria, when oxygen is present. (3)

